{
    "title": "Introduction to a dissertation or research report: physical or life sciences",
    "paragraphs": [
        {
            "structure": {
                "para": [
                    {
                        "id": "2",
                        "text": "Writing an Introduction: physical and life sciences"
                    },
                    {
                        "id": "3",
                        "text": "How to write an effective introduction to your work"
                    },
                    {
                        "id": "4",
                        "text": "TIP The three-move structure will help you write your introduction. Choose only the steps that are appropriate for your study. Moves and steps can be any length from a few words to a whole section of the introduction.",
                        "tip": true
                    }
                ]
            },
            "notes": {
                "para": []
            },
            "content": {
                "para": [],
                "placeHolder": "",
                "id": ""
            }
        },
        {
            "structure": {
                "para": [
                    {
                        "id": "93",
                        "text": "Move 1: Establishing a territory"
                    },
                    {
                        "id": "94",
                        "text": "Move 1 gives the general background to your study."
                    }
                ]
            },
            "notes": {
                "para": []
            },
            "content": {
                "para": [],
                "placeHolder": "",
                "id": ""
            }
        },
        {
            "structure": {
                "para": [
                    {
                        "id": "6",
                        "text": "Step 1"
                    },
                    {
                        "id": "7",
                        "text": "1. Describe why your general study area is relevant or important in the field and/or the world."
                    },
                    {
                        "id": "8",
                        "text": "TIP Use references to support your claims.",
                        "tip": true
                    }
                ]
            },
            "notes": {
                "para": []
            },
            "content": {
                "para": [
                    [
                        {
                            "id": "10",
                            "text": " More than one billion people currently lack access to low-cost drinking water and more than 2.3 billion people live in water-stressed areas. "
                        },
                        {
                            "id": "11",
                            "text": " Thus, it is of paramount importance to develop efficient and cost-effective water purification methods especially for the removal of bacteria and viruses "
                        },
                        {
                            "id": "12",
                            "text": " [1–10] "
                        },
                        {
                            "id": "9",
                            "text": "."
                        }
                    ]
                ],
                "placeHolder": "Type Step 1 here.",
                "id": "9"
            }
        },
        {
            "structure": {
                "para": [
                    {
                        "id": "14",
                        "text": "Steps 2 and 3"
                    },
                    {
                        "id": "15",
                        "text": "2. Make generalizations about your area; give background information or examples. Each generalization becomes narrower until you focus on your own specific topic.\n</br>3. Review previous research."
                    },
                    {
                        "id": "16",
                        "text": "TIP Use references to support your claims.",
                        "tip": true
                    }
                ]
            },
            "notes": {
                "para": []
            },
            "content": {
                "para": [
                    [
                        {
                            "id": "18",
                            "text": " There are many water purification methods, including filtration (size separation), adsorption, chemical coagulation, photodegradation, biodegradation, distillation and active sludge "
                        },
                        {
                            "id": "17",
                            "text": " , "
                        },
                        {
                            "id": "19",
                            "text": " but filtration is the most versatile chemical-free method, and it is extremely cost-effective, time- and energy-saving "
                        },
                        {
                            "id": "20",
                            "text": " [11,12] "
                        },
                        {
                            "id": "17",
                            "text": "."
                        },
                        {
                            "id": "21",
                            "text": " Filtration systems have considerably improved in the past decade and many of them possess complex structures, mechanisms and materials such as porous media, polymer membranes … "
                        },
                        {
                            "id": "22",
                            "text": " The filtration processes are categorized mainly by the average size of the pores in the membranes, which include microfiltration (MF), ultrafiltration (UF), nanofiltration and reverse osmosis. "
                        },
                        {
                            "id": "23",
                            "text": " MF filters have relatively large pores and thus yield a high flux rate compared with UF filters with smaller pores (10–100 nm) "
                        },
                        {
                            "id": "24",
                            "text": " [13,14] "
                        },
                        {
                            "id": "17",
                            "text": "."
                        }
                    ]
                ],
                "placeHolder": "Type Steps 2 and 3 here.",
                "id": "17"
            }
        },
        {
            "structure": {
                "para": []
            },
            "notes": {
                "para": []
            },
            "content": {
                "para": [
                    [
                        {
                            "id": "26",
                            "text": " More than one billion people currently "
                        },
                        {
                            "id": "27",
                            "text": " lack "
                        },
                        {
                            "id": "26",
                            "text": " access to low-cost drinking water and more than 2.3 billion people "
                        },
                        {
                            "id": "28",
                            "text": " live "
                        },
                        {
                            "id": "26",
                            "text": " in water-stressed areas. Thus, it is of paramount importance to develop efficient and cost-effective water purification methods especially for the removal of bacteria and viruses [1–10]. There are many water purification methods, including filtration (size separation), adsorption, chemical coagulation, photodegradation, biodegradation, distillation and active sludge, but filtration "
                        },
                        {
                            "id": "29",
                            "text": " is "
                        },
                        {
                            "id": "26",
                            "text": " the most versatile chemical-free method, and it "
                        },
                        {
                            "id": "30",
                            "text": " is "
                        },
                        {
                            "id": "26",
                            "text": " extremely cost-effective, time- and energy-saving [11,12]. Filtration systems have considerably improved in the past decade and many of them "
                        },
                        {
                            "id": "31",
                            "text": " possess "
                        },
                        {
                            "id": "26",
                            "text": " complex structures, mechanisms and materials such as porous media, polymer membranes … The filtration processes "
                        },
                        {
                            "id": "32",
                            "text": " are categorized "
                        },
                        {
                            "id": "26",
                            "text": " mainly by the average size of the pores in the membranes, which "
                        },
                        {
                            "id": "33",
                            "text": " include "
                        },
                        {
                            "id": "26",
                            "text": " microfiltration (MF), ultrafiltration (UF), nanofiltration and reverse osmosis. MF filters have relatively large pores and thus yield a high flux rate compared with UF filters with smaller pores (10–100nm) [13,14]. An MF membrane with a pore size ranging from 0.1 to 10 μm nominally "
                        },
                        {
                            "id": "46",
                            "text": " is "
                        },
                        {
                            "id": "26",
                            "text": " capable of retaining microbe-like bacteria larger than 0.2 μm in diameter, but this "
                        },
                        {
                            "id": "47",
                            "text": " is "
                        },
                        {
                            "id": "26",
                            "text": " not suitable for removing viruses, which "
                        },
                        {
                            "id": "48",
                            "text": " are "
                        },
                        {
                            "id": "26",
                            "text": " often smaller in size by a factor of 10 [15]. "
                        }
                    ]
                ],
                "placeHolder": "",
                "id": "26",
                "duplicate": true
            }
        },
        {
            "structure": {
                "para": [
                    {
                        "id": "35",
                        "text": "Move 2: Establishing a Niche"
                    },
                    {
                        "id": "36",
                        "text": "Move 2 shows that there is a need for your study, often by establishing that there is a gap or problem in previous research."
                    }
                ]
            },
            "notes": {
                "para": []
            },
            "content": {
                "para": [],
                "placeHolder": "",
                "id": ""
            }
        },
        {
            "structure": {
                "para": [
                    {
                        "id": "38",
                        "text": "Steps 1, 2 and 3"
                    },
                    {
                        "id": "39",
                        "text": "1. Indicate a gap, problem or need in previous research.\n</br>2. Indicate a problem or need in the world.\n</br>3. Continue or extend the work of other researchers."
                    },
                    {
                        "id": "40",
                        "text": "TIP Use Step 1 and/or Step 2. Step 3 is optional. Use references to support your claims.",
                        "tip": true
                    }
                ]
            },
            "notes": {
                "para": []
            },
            "content": {
                "para": [
                    [
                        {
                            "id": "41",
                            "text": " An MF membrane with a pore size ranging from 0.1 to 10 μm nominally is capable of retaining microbe-like bacteria larger than 0.2 μm in diameter, "
                        },
                        {
                            "id": "42",
                            "text": " but this is not suitable for removing viruses, which are often smaller in size by a factor of 10 "
                        },
                        {
                            "id": "43",
                            "text": " [15] "
                        },
                        {
                            "id": "41",
                            "text": "."
                        }
                    ]
                ],
                "placeHolder": "Type Move 2 here.",
                "id": "41"
            }
        },
        {
            "structure": {
                "para": [
                    {
                        "id": "50",
                        "text": "Move 3: Occupying the niche"
                    },
                    {
                        "id": "51",
                        "text": "Move 3 gives details of your study, showing how it fills the gap or deals with the problem."
                    }
                ]
            },
            "notes": {
                "para": []
            },
            "content": {
                "para": [],
                "placeHolder": "",
                "id": ""
            }
        },
        {
            "structure": {
                "para": [
                    {
                        "id": "53",
                        "text": "Steps 1 and 2"
                    },
                    {
                        "id": "54",
                        "text": "1. Present your research and state its purposes, aims or objectives.\n</br>2. State your research questions or hypotheses."
                    },
                    {
                        "id": "55",
                        "text": "Step 2 is optional.",
                        "tip": true
                    }
                ]
            },
            "notes": {
                "para": []
            },
            "content": {
                "para": [
                    [
                        {
                            "id": "57",
                            "text": " In this study, we report a promising method for producing porous membranes that are based on MF but have capabilities for retaining viruses. "
                        }
                    ]
                ],
                "placeHolder": "Type Steps 1 and 2 here.",
                "id": "56"
            }
        },
        {
            "structure": {
                "para": [
                    {
                        "id": "59",
                        "text": "Steps 3 and 4"
                    },
                    {
                        "id": "60",
                        "text": "3. Describe briefly the work you carried out.\n</br>4. Describe briefly the methods, materials or subjects you used."
                    },
                    {
                        "id": "61",
                        "text": "TIP Steps 3 and 4 can come in either order.",
                        "tip": true
                    }
                ]
            },
            "notes": {
                "para": []
            },
            "content": {
                "para": [
                    [
                        {
                            "id": "63",
                            "text": " The membranes are composed of ultra-fine cellulose nanofibers (UFCNs) (5–10 nm in diameter) derived from safe, inexpensive and sustainable nanomaterials such as wood pulp. The cellulose nanofibers were infused into the submicron-sized fibrous scaffolds (100–300 nm in diameter) fabricated by electrospinning. "
                        },
                        {
                            "id": "64",
                            "text": " By controlling the processing parameters and material ingredients, we were able to create a composite fibrous membrane structure that has an appropriate pore size to sieve bacteria and a suitable static charge to adsorb viruses. "
                        }
                    ]
                ],
                "placeHolder": "Type Steps 3 and 4 here.",
                "id": "62"
            }
        },
        {
            "structure": {
                "para": []
            },
            "notes": {
                "para": []
            },
            "content": {
                "para": [
                    [
                        {
